"[2601.09974] SPRInG: Continual LLM Personalization via Selective Parametric Adaptation and Retrieval-Interpolated Generation (https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.09974)\nciteturn30955view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.09974\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 164\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSPRInG: Continual LLM Personalization via Selective Parametric Adaptation and Retrieval-Interpolated Generation (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.09974v1)\nciteturn30955view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.09974v1\",\"pattern\":\"Latency\"}); Total lines: 703\nL262: Our method addresses the limitations of existing CL and retrieval-based approaches by (1) implementing a drift-driven selective adaptation that isolates high-novelty interactions to update user-specific adapters, and (2) employing a gated retrieval-interpolated generation that dynamically balances parametric knowledge with historical evidence at the logit level. Through extensive experiments, we show that SPRInG effectively captures genuine preference drifts while robustly filtering out transient noise.\nL263: Collectively, our findings underscore the importance of distinguishing genuine preference drift from transient contexts, and establish a scalable path for deploying responsive, long-term personalized LLMs in real-world scenarios.\nL264: ## Limitation and Future Work\nL265: While the proposed approach yields substantial gains, it comes with practical trade-offs that offer directions for future research. First, the dual-path logit interpolation strategy introduces inference latency and cost overhead, as it requires two forward passes per decoding step. However, this is partially offset by our selective training strategy, which updates parameters on only 30% of the data, reducing the overall training cost compared to exhaustive retraining.\nL266: To further optimize inference efficiency, future work could explore adaptive interpolation strategies, such as terminating the dual-path process mid-generation or reverting to a single parametric path once the divergence between the two distributions falls below a certain threshold.\nL267: Next, the reliability of the drift score may be challenged by transient noise or abrupt topic shifts (false drift) that do not reflect persistent preference evolution. In our framework, we reduce the impact of such noise by restricting updates to a small, high-novelty subset and preserving hard-but-informative cases in a bounded residual buffer, while inference-time relevance gating additionally prevents noisy retrieved histories from interfering with generation.\nL268: Future research could enhance this by adopting more sophisticated retrieval and gating frameworks, such as dense vector-based retrieval or cross-encoder re-ranking, to more precisely isolate semantically relevant evidence from diverse interaction streams.\nL269: Lastly, scaling user-specific adapters poses an infrastructure challenge as the user base grows. Nevertheless, the parameter-efficient nature of LoRA and modern multi-adapter serving techniques make this approach increasingly viable for large-scale deployment.\nL270: As a potential extension, exploring hierarchical or shared adapter architectures that can group users with similar preference trajectories could alleviate storage burdens while facilitating cross-user knowledge transfer (cite65†Tan et al., 2024 ; cite93†Zhang et al., 2025 ).\nL271: ## Broader Impact and Ethical Implications\nL272: The deployment of SPRInG for continual personalization can improve long-term user experience and reduce wasteful computation by avoiding indiscriminate retraining or unfiltered retrieval, thereby enabling more resource-efficient adaptation.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSPRInG: Continual LLM Personalization via Selective Parametric Adaptation and Retrieval-Interpolated Generation (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.09974v1)\nciteturn30955view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.09974v1\",\"pattern\":\"A.1\"}); Total lines: 703\nL15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Problem Formulation L18:   4. cite8†3 Selective Parametric Adaptation and Retrieval-Interpolated Generation L19:     1. cite9†3.1 Training: Drift-driven selective adaptation and residual replay L20:       1. cite10†Drift sample selection. L21:       2. cite11†Parametric adapter update. L22:       3. cite12†Residual replay buffer construction. L23:     2. cite13†3.2 Inference: Gated replay-augmented generation and logit interpolation L24:       1. cite14†Relevance gating mechanism. L25:       2. cite15†Dual-path logit interpolation. L26:   5. cite16†4 Experiments L27:     1. cite17†4.1 Setups L28:       1. cite18†Datasets and metrics. L29:       2. cite19†Baselines. L30:       3. cite20†Implementation details. L31:     2. cite21†4.2 Main results L32:     3. cite22†4.3 More Analyses L33:       1. cite23†Ablation study on training dynamics. L34:       2. cite24†Ablation study on inference dynamics. L35:       3. cite25†Robustness across the number of interactions. L36:   6. cite26†5 Related Works L37:     1. cite27†Continual learning. L38:   7. cite28†6 Conclusion L39:   8. cite29†References L40:   9. cite30†A Implementation Details L41:     1. cite31†A.1 Training and inference algorithms L42:     2. cite32†A.2 Replay buffer retention policies L43:   10. cite33†B Datasets L44:     1. cite34†B.1 Dataset specification L45:       1. cite35†Abstract Generation. L46:       2. cite36†Review Writing. L47:     2. cite37†B.2 Dataset statistics L48:       1. cite38†User categorization criteria. L49:       2. cite39†Descriptive statistics. L50:   11. cite40†C Baselines L51:   12. cite41†D Extended Ablation Studies L52:     1. cite42†D.1 Training dynamics: drift score analysis L53:       1. cite43†Structural analysis of drift score components. L54:       2. cite44†Robustness to balancing parameter $\\alpha$. L55:       3. cite45†Distinguishing drift score from hard example mining. L56:       4. cite46†Visualization of selected drift samples. L57:     2. cite47†D.2 Inference dynamics: relevance gating L58:   13. cite48†E Qualitative Analysis L59:     1. cite49†E.1 Qualitative comparison with baselines L60:     2. cite50†E.2 Qualitative analysis of interpolation inference L61:   14. cite51†F Prompts L62:     1. cite52†Abstract Generation L63:     2. cite53†Review Writing L64:   15. cite54†G Chat Template L65:   16. cite55†H Usage of AI assistants L66: cite56†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L67: \nL68: arXiv:2601.09974v1 [cs.AI] 15 Jan 2026\nL69: # SPRInG: Continual LLM Personalization via Selective Parametric Adaptation and Retrieval-Interpolated Generation\nL70: \nL71: Seoyeon Kim Affiliation: Yonsei University Email: yeon_04@yonsei.ac.kr    Jaehyung Kim Affiliation: Yonsei University Email: jaehyungk@yonsei.ac.kr\nL72: ###### Abstract\nL73: Personalizing Large Language Models typically relies on static retrieval or one-time adaptation, assuming user preferences remain invariant over time. However, real-world interactions are dynamic, where user interests continuously evolve, posing a challenge for models to adapt to preference drift without catastrophic forgetting.\nL74: Standard continual learning approaches often struggle in this context, as they indiscriminately update on noisy interaction streams, failing to distinguish genuine preference shifts from transient contexts. To address this, we introduce SPRInG, a novel semi-parametric framework designed for effective continual personalization. During training, SPRInG employs drift-driven selective adaptation, which utilizes a likelihood-based scoring function to identify high-novelty interactions.\nL302:   * Zhang et al. (2025) Linhai Zhang, Jialong Wu, Deyu Zhou, and Yulan He. 2025. Proper: A progressive learning framework for personalized large language models with group-level adaptation. In Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL).\nL303:   * Zhang et al. (2024) Zhehao Zhang, Ryan A Rossi, Branislav Kveton, Yijia Shao, Diyi Yang, Hamed Zamani, Franck Dernoncourt, Joe Barrow, Tong Yu, Sungchul Kim, and 1 others. 2024. Personalization of large language models: A survey. arXiv preprint arXiv:2411.00027.\nL304: ## Appendix A Implementation Details\nL305: ### A.1 Training and inference algorithms\nL306: \nL307: Algorithm 1 SPRInG training algorithm\nL308: \nL309: Input: Base LLM $\\mathcal{M}_{\\theta}$ (frozen), initial user adapter $\\phi_{u}^{(0)}$, interaction stream $\\{\\mathcal{D}_{u}^{(t)}\\}_{t\\geq 1}$, scoring function $\\mathcal{S}(\\cdot)$ (Eq. cite85†1 ), top percentile $p$, buffer budget $N_{\\max}$\nL310: \nL311: $\\mathcal{B}_{u}^{(0)}\\leftarrow\\emptyset$\nL312: \nL313: for $t=1,2,\\cdots$ do\nL314: \nL315:    /* Drift sample selection */\nL316: \nL317:   for $(q,a)\\in\\mathcal{D}_{u}^{(t)}$ do\nL318:    $\\mathcal{S}_{t}(q,a)\\leftarrow\\mathcal{S}(q,a;\\mathcal{M}_{\\theta},\\phi_{u}^{(t-1)})$\nL319: \nL320:   end for\nL321: \nL322:   $\\mathcal{D}_{\\texttt{drift}}^{(t)}\\leftarrow\\{(q,a)\\in\\mathcal{D}_{u}^{(t)}~|~\\mathcal{S}_{t}(q,a)\\geq\\tau_{p}\\}$\nL323: \nL324:    /* Parametric adapter update */\nL325: \nL326:   $\\phi_{u}^{(t)}\\leftarrow\\phi_{u}^{(t-1)}\\oplus\\mathcal{D}_{\\texttt{drift}}^{(t)}$\nL327: \nL328:    /* Replay buffer construction */\nL329: \nL330:   $\\mathcal{C}_{u}^{(t)}\\leftarrow\\mathcal{D}_{\\texttt{drift}}^{(t)}\\cup\\mathcal{B}_{u}^{(t-1)}$\nL331:   for $(q,a)\\in\\mathcal{C}_{u}^{(t)}$ do\nL332: \nL333:    $\\mathcal{S}^{\\prime}_{t}(q,a)\\leftarrow\\mathcal{S}(q,a;\\mathcal{M}_{\\theta},\\phi_{u}^{(t)})$\nL334: \nL335:   end for\nL336: \nL337:   $\\mathcal{B}_{u}^{(t)}\\leftarrow\\operatorname*{Top-N_{\\max}}_{(q,a)\\in\\mathcal{C}_{u}^{(t)}}\\left(\\mathcal{S}^{\\prime}_{t}(q,a)\\right)$\nL338: \nL339: end for\nL340: \nL341: return period-wise adapters $\\{\\phi_{u}^{(t)}\\}$ and replay buffers $\\{\\mathcal{B}_{u}^{(t)}\\}$\nL342: Algorithm cite74†1 presents the training pipeline for SPRInG. It identifies drift samples based on the scoring function (Eq. cite85†1 ), updates both the parametric adapter, and the replay buffer within the budget $N_{\\max}$.\nL343: \nL344: Algorithm 2 SPRInG inference algorithm\nL345: \nL346: Input: User-adapted model $\\mathcal{M}_{u}^{(t)}$, replay buffer $\\mathcal{B}_{u}^{(t)}$, test query $q$, retrieval top-$k$, relevance threshold $\\sigma$, interpolation weight $\\lambda$\nL347: \nL348: /* Relevance gated retrieval */\nL349: $\\mathcal{R}_{q}\\leftarrow\\text{Top-}k\\{h\\in\\mathcal{B}_{u}^{(t)}\\mid\\textsc{Sim}(q,h)\\}$\nL350: \nL351: $\\mathbf{c}_{\\texttt{ret}}\\leftarrow\\textsc{Concat}\\{h\\in\\mathcal{R}_{q}\\mid\\textsc{Sim}(q,h)>\\sigma\\}$\nL352: \nL353: /* Logit interpolation */\nL354: \nL355: $a_{<1}\\leftarrow\\emptyset$\nL356: \nL357: for $j=1,2,\\cdots$ until EOS do\nL358: \nL359:   Compute $p_{\\texttt{vanilla}}$ and $p_{\\texttt{RAG}}$ (Eqs. cite97†6 , cite98†7 )\nL360: \nL361:   Compute $P_{\\texttt{final}}$ by interpolation (Eq. cite87†8 )\nL362: \nL363:   $\\hat{a}_{j}\\sim P_{\\texttt{final}}(\\cdot)$\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSPRInG: Continual LLM Personalization via Selective Parametric Adaptation and Retrieval-Interpolated Generation (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.09974v1)\nciteturn30955view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.09974v1\",\"pattern\":\"Limitations\"}); Total lines: 703\nL259: Conversely, replay-based methods maintain a buffer to interleave past examples with current streams. To maximize efficiency, recent work prioritizes selective data retention over uniform sampling (cite94†Rolnick et al., 2019 ), utilizing metrics like interference (cite95†Aljundi et al., 2019 ), confidence scores (cite72†Kim et al., 2024 ), activation magnitudes (cite69†Du et al., 2024 ), or spaced repetition heuristics (cite96†M’hamdi and May, 2024 ) to identify the most informative samples.\nL260: ## 6 Conclusion\nL261: In this paper, we propose SPRInG, a semi-parametric framework that accounts for the challenges arising from evolving user preferences in continual LLM personalization.\nL262: Our method addresses the limitations of existing CL and retrieval-based approaches by (1) implementing a drift-driven selective adaptation that isolates high-novelty interactions to update user-specific adapters, and (2) employing a gated retrieval-interpolated generation that dynamically balances parametric knowledge with historical evidence at the logit level. Through extensive experiments, we show that SPRInG effectively captures genuine preference drifts while robustly filtering out transient noise.\nL263: Collectively, our findings underscore the importance of distinguishing genuine preference drift from transient contexts, and establish a scalable path for deploying responsive, long-term personalized LLMs in real-world scenarios.\nL264: ## Limitation and Future Work\nL265: While the proposed approach yields substantial gains, it comes with practical trade-offs that offer directions for future research. First, the dual-path logit interpolation strategy introduces inference latency and cost overhead, as it requires two forward passes per decoding step. However, this is partially offset by our selective training strategy, which updates parameters on only 30% of the data, reducing the overall training cost compared to exhaustive retraining.\nL266: To further optimize inference efficiency, future work could explore adaptive interpolation strategies, such as terminating the dual-path process mid-generation or reverting to a single parametric path once the divergence between the two distributions falls below a certain threshold.\nL267: Next, the reliability of the drift score may be challenged by transient noise or abrupt topic shifts (false drift) that do not reflect persistent preference evolution. In our framework, we reduce the impact of such noise by restricting updates to a small, high-novelty subset and preserving hard-but-informative cases in a bounded residual buffer, while inference-time relevance gating additionally prevents noisy retrieved histories from interfering with generation.\nL268: Future research could enhance this by adopting more sophisticated retrieval and gating frameworks, such as dense vector-based retrieval or cross-encoder re-ranking, to more precisely isolate semantically relevant evidence from diverse interaction streams.\n"
